  <section class="part prose">
    <h2>So what is type 1 diabetes?</h2>
    <p>Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune condition. The body's immune system, which normally fights germs, mistakenly attacks the cells in the pancreas that make insulin.</p>
    <p>Once those cells are gone, the body makes little or no insulin. Without it, sugar from food stays in the blood instead of fuelling the body. Over time, high blood sugar can harm the eyes, kidneys, nerves and heart, which is why it has to be managed every day.</p>
    <p>Nobody knows exactly what triggers it. It isn't caused by eating too much sugar or by anything a person did, and it can't be prevented. It often starts in childhood or the teenage years, but it can appear at any age.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="part">
    <h2>Type 1 isn't the same as type 2</h2>
    <div class="compare">
      <div>
        <h3>Type 1</h3>
        <p>The body makes little or no insulin. It's autoimmune, often starts young, and always needs insulin.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Type 2</h3>
        <p>The body still makes insulin but doesn't respond to it well. It's far more common, linked to genes and lifestyle, and is often managed with diet, exercise and tablets.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="part">
    <h2>True or false?</h2>
    <p class="prose">Tap a card to flip it. Most people get at least one of these wrong.</p>
    <div class="myths">
      <button type="button" class="myth" aria-pressed="false">
        <span class="k">1 of 6</span>
        <span class="front"><span class="q">“Eating too much sugar causes type 1.”</span><span class="tap">Tap to flip</span></span>
        <span class="back"><span class="verdict">False.</span><span class="a">Type 1 is an autoimmune condition. Nothing a person ate or did causes it.</span><span class="tap">“Eating too much sugar causes type 1.”</span></span>
      </button>
      <button type="button" class="myth" aria-pressed="false">
        <span class="k">2 of 6</span>
        <span class="front"><span class="q">“People with type 1 can never eat sweets.”</span><span class="tap">Tap to flip</span></span>
        <span class="back"><span class="verdict">False.</span><span class="a">They can eat sweets. They match the insulin they take to the food, the same job a pancreas does on its own.</span><span class="tap">“People with type 1 can never eat sweets.”</span></span>
      </button>
      <button type="button" class="myth" aria-pressed="false">
        <span class="k">3 of 6</span>
        <span class="front"><span class="q">“Kids grow out of it.”</span><span class="tap">Tap to flip</span></span>
        <span class="back"><span class="verdict">False.</span><span class="a">Type 1 is lifelong. The cells that make insulin don't come back.</span><span class="tap">“Kids grow out of it.”</span></span>
      </button>
      <button type="button" class="myth" aria-pressed="false">
        <span class="k">4 of 6</span>
        <span class="front"><span class="q">“You can catch it from someone.”</span><span class="tap">Tap to flip</span></span>
        <span class="back"><span class="verdict">False.</span><span class="a">Type 1 isn't contagious. It can't pass from one person to another.</span><span class="tap">“You can catch it from someone.”</span></span>
      </button>
      <button type="button" class="myth" aria-pressed="false">
