  <section class="part">
    <h2>Living with it</h2>
    <p class="prose">There's no cure yet, but type 1 is very manageable. Day to day, it comes down to three things.</p>
    <ul class="living">
      <li>
        <svg width="48" height="48" viewBox="0 0 48 48" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="8" y="20" width="28" height="9" rx="4.5" fill="none" stroke="#1B2430" stroke-width="2.5"/><rect x="14" y="20" width="10" height="9" fill="#F2A93B"/><path d="M36 24.5h6" stroke="#1B2430" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"/></svg>
        <div>
          <h3>Taking insulin</h3>
          <p>Several injections a day with an insulin pen, or a small pump worn on the body that delivers it automatically.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <svg width="48" height="48" viewBox="0 0 48 48" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="24" cy="24" r="15" fill="none" stroke="#1B2430" stroke-width="2.5"/><circle cx="24" cy="24" r="5" fill="#2F5BEA"/></svg>
        <div>
          <h3>Checking blood sugar</h3>
          <p>With a finger-prick meter, or a sensor on the arm that reads sugar levels around the clock and sends them to a phone.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <svg width="48" height="48" viewBox="0 0 48 48" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 32c5-10 10-10 16 0s11 10 16 0" fill="none" stroke="#1B2430" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"/><circle cx="16" cy="18" r="3" fill="#F2A93B"/><circle cx="32" cy="18" r="3" fill="#2F5BEA"/></svg>
        <div>
          <h3>Balancing food and activity</h3>
          <p>Matching insulin to meals and exercise. Too little insulin means high sugar; too much can cause a low, which is fixed quickly with juice or glucose tablets.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

