  <section class="part" id="low">
    <h2>If someone is having a low</h2>
    <p class="prose">Low blood sugar comes on fast and needs sugar straight away. Anyone can help.</p>
    <ul class="low-signs">
      <li class="label">Look for</li>
      <li>Shaky</li><li>Sweaty</li><li>Pale</li><li>Confused or irritable</li><li>Suddenly very hungry</li>
    </ul>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h3>Give fast sugar</h3><p>Half a cup (about 120 ml) of juice or regular (not diet) soda, or 3 to 4 glucose tablets. That's about 15 grams of sugar.</p></li>
      <li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">2</span><h3>Wait 15 minutes</h3><p>Stay with them and have them sit down. Then they check their blood sugar again.</p></li>
      <li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">3</span><h3>Still low? Repeat</h3><p>Give the same amount again. Once they feel better, a snack helps keep it steady.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <div class="emergency">
      <svg width="48" height="48" viewBox="0 0 52 52" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="26" cy="26" r="24" fill="#F2A93B"/><rect x="23" y="12" width="6" height="19" rx="3" fill="#1B2430"/><circle cx="26" cy="38" r="3.5" fill="#1B2430"/></svg>
      <div>
        <h3>If they pass out or can't swallow</h3>
        <p><strong>Don't give food or drink.</strong> Lay them on their side and call your local medical emergency number. If they carry glucagon and you know how to give it, use it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <a class="helper-link" href="low-helper.html">
      <span><b>Happening right now?</b> Open the Low helper. It walks you through each step and runs the 15-minute timer.</span>
      <svg width="26" height="26" viewBox="0 0 26 26" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 13h15M13.5 6l7 7-7 7" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
    </a>
    <p class="fineprint">This is general first-aid information, not medical advice. If the person has their own care plan, follow that. If you are unsure, call your local medical emergency number.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="part">
    <h2>Signs to know</h2>
    <p class="prose">Type 1 can come on over a few weeks. Four early signs are easy to remember:</p>
    <div class="signs">
      <div class="sign"><b>Toilet</b><span>needing to pee a lot</span></div>
      <div class="sign"><b>Thirsty</b><span>always feeling dry</span></div>
      <div class="sign"><b>Tired</b><span>wiped out for no reason</span></div>
      <div class="sign"><b>Thinner</b><span>losing weight without trying</span></div>
    </div>
    <p class="prose">If you notice these, especially in a child, see a doctor the same day. A quick blood or urine test is the first check, and if it shows high sugar, a hospital confirms it with more tests.</p>
